<span id="motivation"></span>
===Motivation===

A large body of deployed protocols share the same pattern: a sender performs a one-shot ECDH to a recipient's long-term public <code>secp256k1</code> key, derives a secret from it, and publishes data that depends on that secret.

As a CRQC breaks ECDH with Shor's algorithm, every such protocol is exposed to a '''harvest-now-decrypt-later''' attack: an adversary records the published material today and, once a CRQC exists, recovers the shared secret and everything derived from it.
The exposure is retroactive and irreversible.
No key rotation, protocol upgrade, or later migration repairs data that has already been published.
Hence, the confidentiality of such protocols is at permanent risk and a post-quantum upgrade is needed as soon as possible.

One proposed remedy is a ''hybrid key encapsulation'': a shared secret is combined from two components:

* classical ECDH key exchange
* post-quantum key encapsulation mechanism (post-quantum KEM, e.g. ML-KEM)

If one of the two components is compromised, the scheme remains secure, e.g. if advances in cryptanalysis of lattice-based constructions compromise ML-KEM, classical security will still be guaranteed by ECDH.
For this reason, messaging protocols facing the same exposure are actively moving to hybrid key encapsulation (e.g. Signal's [https://signal.org/docs/specifications/pqxdh/ PQXDH] and Apple's [https://security.apple.com/blog/imessage-pq3/ iMessage PQ3]).
For Bitcoin's encrypted peer-to-peer transport protocol [https://github.com/bitcoin/bips/blob/master/bip-0324.mediawiki BIP324], which uses <code>secp256k1</code> ECDH, a hybrid replacement is under discussion on the [https://groups.google.com/g/bitcoindev/c/n_5WuKVYqwI bitcoindev mailing list].

One possible important application of HKE is the [https://blockstream.com/bitcoin17-final41.pdf Confidential Assets] protocol, currently deployed on the Liquid network: sender and recipient establish a shared secret via ECDH and use it to derive a seed for a witness-recoverable range proof, letting the recipient later recover the amount and blinding factors under that same secret.
Range proofs are published on-chain, so an attacker with a CRQC who has seen a recipient's address can later break the ''confidentiality'' of every output sent to it (see [[#confidential-assets|Confidential Assets]]).

==Description==

A recipient holds a 32-byte secret <code>msk</code> and publishes a public key <code>(B, ek)</code>: a <code>secp256k1</code> point and an <code>ML-KEM-768</code> encapsulation key.
A sender who knows the public key produces a 32-byte shared secret <code>K</code> and a ciphertext <code>(E, c)</code>, made of an ephemeral point and an ML-KEM ciphertext.
The recipient recomputes <code>K</code> from <code>(E, c)</code> with <code>msk</code>.
<code>K</code> is one hash over both shared secrets, both ciphertexts and both public keys:

<pre>K := H_seed(K_q || K_c || c || E || ek || B)</pre>

where <code>K_c</code> comes from the ECDH between <code>E</code> and <code>B</code>, and <code>K_q</code> from ML-KEM.

More generally, HKE is an instance of '''UG''', the framework that the CFRG draft [https://datatracker.ietf.org/doc/html/draft-irtf-cfrg-hybrid-kems-12/ ''Hybrid PQ/T Key Encapsulation Mechanisms''] defines for combining a post-quantum KEM with a Diffie–Hellman group.
HKE instantiates it with <code>ML-KEM-768</code>, <code>secp256k1</code> and tagged SHA-256 (see [[#rationale|Rationale]]).

Both parts of the recipient's key are derived from one 32-byte secret <code>msk</code> with separately tagged hashes, so recovering the classical scalar from <code>B</code> with a CRQC does not reveal the ML-KEM decapsulation key.

==Specification==

===Notation===

* The group is <code>secp256k1</code> ([https://www.secg.org/sec2-v2.pdf SEC2], §2.4.1): the points of an elliptic curve over the integers modulo the prime <code>p</code>, a group of prime order <code>n</code> with generator <code>G</code>. <code>k*P</code> is scalar multiplication; <code>O</code> is the point at infinity.
* <code>cbytes(P)</code>: the 33-byte compressed SEC1 encoding of a point <code>P</code> ([https://www.secg.org/sec1-v2.pdf SEC1], §2.3.3).
* <code>int(x)</code>: the integer with big-endian encoding <code>x</code>.
* <code>|x|</code>: the length of the byte string <code>x</code> in bytes.
* <code>x[i]</code>: the byte of <code>x</code> at index <code>i</code>, counting from 0; <code>x[i:j]</code>: the bytes of <code>x</code> at indices <code>i</code> to <code>j-1</code>.
* <code>lift(x)</code>: the point <code>P</code> with <code>cbytes(P) = x</code>. It fails unless <code>|x| = 33</code>, <code>x[0]</code> is <code>0x02</code> or <code>0x03</code>, <code>X = int(x[1:33]) < p</code>, and <code>X</code> is the x coordinate of a point on the curve. <code>X</code> MUST NOT be reduced modulo <code>p</code>. If these hold, <code>P</code> is the point with x coordinate <code>X</code> whose <code>Y</code> is even for <code>0x02</code> and odd for <code>0x03</code>. <code>lift</code> never returns <code>O</code>, and <code>cbytes(lift(x)) = x</code> whenever <code>lift</code> succeeds.
* '''Tagged hash''', as defined in [https://github.com/bitcoin/bips/blob/master/bip-0340.mediawiki BIP340]. For an ASCII tag <code>t</code>, <code>H_t(x) = SHA256(SHA256(t) || SHA256(t) || x)</code>.
* <code>||</code> is concatenation of byte strings.
* <code>x $= S</code>: draw uniformly random <code>x</code> from the set <code>S</code>. A 256-bit string drawn from <code>{0,1}^256</code> is treated as a 32-byte string.
* <code>K</code>: the 32-byte shared secret that HKE delivers to an application. <code>K_c</code>, <code>K_q</code>: the classical (ECDH) and post-quantum (ML-KEM) shared secrets that <code>H_seed</code> combines into <code>K</code>.

This document uses the following tagged hash functions:

{| class="wikitable"
|-
! Function
! Tag <code>t</code>
! Use
|-
| <code>H_blind</code>
| <code>ELIP-HKE/v1/blind</code>
| classical scalar <code>b</code>
|-
| <code>H_kem-d</code>
