This document specifies one parameter set, HKE v1, with <code>ML-KEM-768</code>.
Another parameter set would replace <code>v1</code> in every tag, so that its keys and shared secrets are independent of those of HKE v1, even for the same <code>msk</code>.

In the terms of [https://datatracker.ietf.org/doc/html/draft-irtf-cfrg-hybrid-kems-12#section-5.3 UG], the seed is <code>msk</code>, <code>PRG(msk) = H_kem-d(msk) || H_kem-z(msk) || H_blind(msk)</code>, <code>RandomScalar(s) = int(s) mod n</code>, <code>ElementToSharedSecret(P) = H_ecdh(cbytes(P))</code>, <code>KDF = H_seed</code>, and <code>Label</code> is empty (see [[#secp256k1-and-sha-256|secp256k1 and SHA-256]]).

===KEM interface===

HKE v1 instantiates post-quantum KEM with <code>ML-KEM-768</code>, the category-3 parameter set of [https://csrc.nist.gov/pubs/fips/203/final FIPS 203].
More specifically, HKE's post-quantum <code>KEM</code> instantiation is defined as follows (algorithms and paragraph numbers refer to FIPS 203):

* <code>KEM.KeyGen_internal(d, z) -> (ek, dk)</code> is <code>ML-KEM.KeyGen_internal</code> (Algorithm 16). From the 32-byte seeds <code>d</code> and <code>z</code>, it computes the encapsulation key <code>ek</code> of <code>L_ek = 1184</code> bytes and the decapsulation key <code>dk</code>.
* <code>KEM.Encaps(ek) -> (K_q, c)</code> is <code>ML-KEM.Encaps</code> (Algorithm 20), and <code>KEM.Encaps_internal(ek, m) -> (K_q, c)</code> is <code>ML-KEM.Encaps_internal</code> (Algorithm 17), which takes the 32 bytes of randomness <code>m</code> as input instead of drawing them. Both return the 32-byte shared secret <code>K_q</code> and the ciphertext <code>c</code> of <code>L_c = 1088</code> bytes.
* <code>KEM.Decaps(dk, c) -> K_q</code> is <code>ML-KEM.Decaps</code> (Algorithm 21).
* <code>KEM.CheckEK(ek) -> bool</code> is the encapsulation key check (§7.2). It returns true iff <code>|ek| = L_ek</code> and every 12-bit coefficient decoded from the first <code>L_ek - 32</code> bytes of <code>ek</code> is less than <code>q = 3329</code>.

===Key generation===

<pre>expandDecapsulationKey(msk):
    b := int(H_blind(msk)) mod n; fail if b = 0
    B := b*G
    (ek, dk) := KEM.KeyGen_internal(H_kem-d(msk), H_kem-z(msk))
    Return (b, dk, B, ek)

KeyGen():
    msk $= {0,1}^256
    (_, _, B, ek) := expandDecapsulationKey(msk)
    Return (msk, (cbytes(B), ek))</pre>

A recipient first calls <code>KeyGen</code> to generate a key pair:

* <code>msk</code> (master secret key): the decapsulation key, which the recipient keeps secret.
* <code>(B_bytes, ek)</code>: the public key, which the recipient publishes (e.g. via their address); <code>B_bytes = cbytes(B)</code> is 33 bytes and <code>ek</code> is <code>L_ek</code> bytes.

<code>KeyGen</code> draws <code>msk</code> uniformly at random to keep the description self-contained.
In practice, wallets derive <code>msk</code> deterministically under the key derivation rules of the application, so that a wallet restored from its backup recomputes <code>msk</code> and recovers its outputs.
This document does not fix that derivation.
The derivation MUST be a pseudorandom function, such as HMAC-SHA256, keyed with a secret that is used for nothing else and that an adversary with a CRQC cannot compute.
<code>msk</code> MUST NOT be used outside HKE.
Under these conditions the [[#security|Security]] argument holds unchanged.
For example, a Liquid wallet with the master blinding key <code>mbk</code> according to [https://github.com/satoshilabs/slips/blob/master/slip-0077.md SLIP-77] can set <code>msk := HMAC-SHA256(H_mbk(mbk), scriptPubKey)</code>, where <code>H_mbk</code> is the tagged hash with tag <code>ELIP-HKE/v1/mbk</code>.
Keying HMAC with <code>mbk</code> itself would not do: that gives the SLIP-77 blinding key of the scriptPubKey, whose public key appears in the wallet's confidential addresses.

===Encapsulation===

Input: recipient public key <code>(B_bytes, ek)</code>, as byte strings.
Output: shared secret <code>K</code> and ciphertext <code>(E_bytes, c)</code>.

<pre>Encaps(B_bytes, ek):
    e $= [1, n-1]
    m $= {0,1}^256
    Return EncapsDerand(B_bytes, ek, e, m)

EncapsDerand(B_bytes, ek, e, m):
    B := lift(B_bytes); fail if lift fails
    Fail if not KEM.CheckEK(ek)
    Fail if not 1 <= e <= n-1
    Fail if |m| != 32
    E := e*G
    E_bytes := cbytes(E)
    K_c := H_ecdh(cbytes(e*B))
    (K_q, c) := KEM.Encaps_internal(ek, m)
    K := H_seed(K_q || K_c || c || E_bytes || ek || B_bytes)
    Return (K, (E_bytes, c))</pre>

We give the version of <code>Encaps</code> which takes the control of the randomness generation to allow the generation of test vectors.
Real implementations MUST instead use <code>Encaps</code> that computes <code>(K_q, c) := KEM.Encaps(ek)</code>, which draws <code>m</code> itself.

A sender publishes <code>E_bytes</code> and <code>c</code> for the recipient (e.g. in a transaction output) and uses <code>K</code> to encrypt data to the recipient (e.g. in Liquid it seeds the DRBG for witness-recoverable range proofs).
An application that needs several keys from one encapsulation MUST derive them from <code>K</code> with domain separation, for example as <code>H_t(K)</code> with a different tag <code>t</code> for each key, rather than use <code>K</code> itself for more than one purpose.

<span id="decapsulation"></span>
===Decapsulation===

Input: ciphertext <code>(E_bytes, c)</code> and the 32-byte decapsulation key <code>msk</code>.
Output: shared secret <code>K</code>.

<pre>Decaps((E_bytes, c), msk):
    (b, dk, B, ek) := expandDecapsulationKey(msk)
    E := lift(E_bytes); fail if lift fails
    Fail if |c| != L_c
    K_c := H_ecdh(cbytes(b*E))
    K_q := KEM.Decaps(dk, c)
    K := H_seed(K_q || K_c || c || E_bytes || ek || cbytes(B))
    Return K</pre>

<code>Decaps</code> fails in three cases only: <code>lift(E_bytes)</code> fails, <code>|c| != L_c</code>, or <code>b = 0</code> in <code>expandDecapsulationKey</code>, which happens with probability <code>2^-255</code>.
If <code>(E_bytes, c)</code> was not produced by <code>Encaps</code> for this public key, <code>Decaps</code> still returns a <code>K</code>, unrelated to the sender's: any change to <code>E_bytes</code> or <code>c</code> changes the input to <code>H_seed</code>, and <code>KEM.Decaps</code> uses implicit rejection, returning a pseudorandom <code>K_q</code> for a <code>c</code> not produced for <code>ek</code>.
The recipient finds out only when the application tries to use <code>K</code>: in [[#confidential-assets|Confidential Assets]], the range-proof rewind fails.
An application MUST treat a <code>K</code> that fails in use as a ciphertext not addressed to it, not as an error.

<span id="security"></span>
==Security==

HKE is an instance of UG, so the IND-CCA analyses that the CFRG draft gives for UG ([https://datatracker.ietf.org/doc/html/draft-irtf-cfrg-hybrid-kems-12#section-6.4.1 §6.4.1], detailed in [https://doi.org/10.62056/ahmp-49p1 Connolly and Grubbs]) apply to it.
Informally, with the tagged hashes modeled as random oracles, <code>K</code> is indistinguishable from random if either of the following holds:

# '''<code>ML-KEM-768</code> is IND-CCA secure.''' This covers quantum adversaries, with quantum access to the random oracles.
# '''The strong Diffie–Hellman problem on <code>secp256k1</code> is hard.''' This protects <code>K</code> against classical adversaries if <code>ML-KEM-768</code> is broken: computing <code>K_c</code> requires <code>e*B</code> from <code>B</code> and <code>E</code>. As in [https://eprint.iacr.org/2024/039 X-Wing], a hybrid of <code>X25519</code> and <code>ML-KEM-768</code>, the proof answers decapsulation queries with a strong Diffie–Hellman oracle.

[[#combiner|Combiner]] compares these conditions with those of X-Wing.

<code>H_seed</code> hashes the whole ciphertext and the whole public key, so <code>K</code> is also binding if <code>H_seed</code> is collision resistant: even an adversary that knows the decapsulation keys cannot make two different ciphertexts, or ciphertexts for two different public keys, decapsulate to the same <code>K</code>, since that would give two different <code>H_seed</code> inputs with the same output (LEAK-BIND-K-CT and LEAK-BIND-K-PK).
The CFRG draft makes this argument for UG informally ([https://datatracker.ietf.org/doc/html/draft-irtf-cfrg-hybrid-kems-12#section-6.4.2.1 §6.4.2.1]) and defers rigorous proofs to a forthcoming paper.

HKE does not provide forward secrecy (compromise of <code>msk</code> exposes every <code>K</code> sent to it), sender authentication, deniability, or an explicit failure signal (see [[#decapsulation|Decapsulation]]).
It also makes no claim that a ciphertext hides the public key it was made for (recipient anonymity).

Implementations SHOULD perform every operation on secret values (<code>msk</code>, <code>b</code>, <code>dk</code>, <code>e</code>, <code>m</code>, <code>K_c</code>, <code>K_q</code> and <code>K</code>) in constant time, in particular <code>b*E</code> and <code>KEM.Decaps</code>, which <code>Decaps</code> runs on ciphertexts that anyone can send to the recipient.
The reference implementation is not constant time.

<span id="rationale"></span>
==Rationale==

HKE is designed for Bitcoin, Liquid, and protocols that use the same primitives, such as Nostr.
These use <code>secp256k1</code> for keys and SHA-256 for hashing, so HKE keeps both and adds only what they lack, a post-quantum KEM.

<span id="combiner"></span>
===Combiner===

HKE uses UG's combiner rather than X-Wing's (the CFRG draft's CG) to avoid an extra assumption on <code>ML-KEM-768</code>.
[https://eprint.iacr.org/2024/039 X-Wing] does not hash the ML-KEM ciphertext <code>c</code>, so its classical security (Theorem 1) also needs <code>ML-KEM-768</code> to be ciphertext second-preimage resistant (C2PRI).
UG hashes <code>c</code>, so the classical condition of [[#security|Security]] needs nothing from <code>ML-KEM-768</code>.
The price is about 2.3 KB of SHA-256 input per encapsulation, for <code>c</code> and <code>ek</code>.

[https://csrc.nist.gov/pubs/sp/800/227/final NIST SP 800-227] (§4.6.3) gives this form of combiner as its example of one that preserves IND-CCA security, citing [https://eprint.iacr.org/2018/024 Giacon, Heuer and Poettering].
<code>H_ecdh</code> turns the point <code>e*B</code> into the 32-byte <code>K_c</code> by hashing its compressed encoding, as the ECDH module of <code>libsecp256k1</code> and BIP352 already do.

<span id="secp256k1-and-sha-256"></span>
===secp256k1 and SHA-256===

With <code>secp256k1</code>, the classical part of HKE is the ECDH these protocols already perform, with the same point encodings and the same implementations, such as [https://github.com/bitcoin-core/secp256k1 <code>libsecp256k1</code>] and [https://github.com/BlockstreamResearch/secp256k1-zkp <code>secp256k1-zkp</code>].
A wallet that adopts HKE needs no second curve.
Every hash in HKE is a tagged hash, the domain-separated SHA-256 that BIP340 defines, [https://github.com/bitcoin/bips/blob/master/bip-0341.mediawiki BIP341] and [https://github.com/bitcoin/bips/blob/master/bip-0352.mediawiki BIP352] use, and <code>libsecp256k1</code> implements.

<code>H_seed</code> serves as UG's <code>KDF</code>, which the draft requires to be indifferentiable from a random oracle, even to a quantum attacker (§6.1.5).
SHA-256 does not meet this for arbitrary inputs, because of length extension ([https://doi.org/10.1007/11535218_26 Coron et al.]).
Each tagged hash in HKE, however, is applied only to inputs of one length (2402 bytes for <code>H_seed</code>), and inputs of one length are prefix-free.
On prefix-free inputs, Merkle–Damgård is indifferentiable from a random oracle if its compression function is ideal ([https://doi.org/10.1007/11535218_26 Coron et al.]; [https://eprint.iacr.org/2018/276 Zhandry] against quantum attackers).
In the draft, UG's combiner ends with a label that keeps the KDFs of different instantiations apart (§6.5.1); in HKE, the tag of <code>H_seed</code> already does this, so the label is empty.

===ML-KEM-768===

The encapsulation key <code>ek</code> is part of the recipient's public key, and a ciphertext <code>c</code> is published with every encapsulation, so their sizes are the main criterion for choosing the KEM.
<code>ML-KEM</code> ([https://csrc.nist.gov/pubs/fips/203/final FIPS 203]) has smaller keys and ciphertexts than [https://pqc-hqc.org/doc/hqc_specifications_2025_08_22.pdf HQC], the only other KEM [https://csrc.nist.gov/pubs/ir/8545/final NIST has selected], which at category 3 has a 4514-byte <code>ek</code> and an 8978-byte <code>c</code>.
[https://eprint.iacr.org/2018/383 CSIDH], an isogeny-based key exchange with 64-byte public keys at its original 512-bit parameters, needs public values of about 1 KB at category 3 ([https://eprint.iacr.org/2020/1520 SQALE]), close to the 1184-byte <code>ek</code> and 1088-byte <code>c</code> of <code>ML-KEM-768</code>, and its group action is orders of magnitude slower.

HKE uses <code>ML-KEM-768</code> (category 3), which FIPS 203 (§8) recommends as the default parameter set because it &quot;provides a large security margin at a reasonable performance cost&quot;.
X-Wing, which targets category 1, also uses <code>ML-KEM-768</code> rather than <code>ML-KEM-512</code>, as a hedge against future cryptanalysis ([https://datatracker.ietf.org/doc/html/draft-connolly-cfrg-xwing-kem-11#section-1.2 X-Wing draft], §1.2).
The margin matters for HKE because a ciphertext, once published, stays exposed to any later attack ([[#motivation|Motivation]]).
<code>ML-KEM-1024</code> (category 5) would add 384 bytes to <code>ek</code> and 480 bytes to every <code>c</code>.

==Applications==

HKE fits a protocol when the following conditions hold.

# '''The protocol already uses <code>secp256k1</code> for its classical key exchange.''' If not, use X-Wing directly; HKE exists to reuse the curve and key infrastructure that is already deployed, not to introduce a second curve.
# '''The exchange is one-shot and non-interactive.''' If the parties can complete a round trip, use a hybrid handshake with forward secrecy instead.
# '''The adversary can store the published data until a CRQC exists.''' Harvest-now-decrypt-later only works if the attacker keeps the transcript, so HKE helps more the more public and long-lived that data is. On-chain data is public and permanent, making HKE the ideal choice for replacing the original protocol.
# '''There is somewhere to put ~1.2 KB per public key and ~1.1 KB per encapsulation, and paying for it is affordable at the protocol's natural frequency.''' A KEM ciphertext has no second use in the protocol that carries it, so it is pure overhead. Whether that overhead is acceptable depends far more on how ''often'' it is paid than on its size.

<span id="confidential-assets"></span>
===Confidential Assets===

Every confidential output on Liquid ([https://blockstream.com/bitcoin17-final41.pdf Confidential Assets]) carries an ephemeral public key <code>E</code> and derives its range-proof seed from <code>ECDH(e, B)</code>, where <code>B</code> is the recipient's long-term blinding key.
<code>E</code> is on chain and <code>B</code> is in the recipient's address, so an attacker with a CRQC who has seen the address recovers the seed of every recorded output sent to that address and, by rewinding its range proof, the amount, the asset and both blinding factors: the protocol is exposed to harvest-now-decrypt-later attacks.

Replacing <code>ECDH(e, B)</code> with HKE and using its shared secret <code>K</code> as the range-proof seed requires the recipient's address to carry the ML-KEM encapsulation key <code>ek</code> alongside <code>B</code>, and each confidential output to carry the ciphertext <code>c</code> alongside <code>E</code>.
The range-proof code is unchanged, since it takes a 32-byte seed either way.

===BIP47 reusable payment codes===

In [https://github.com/bitcoin/bips/blob/master/bip-0047.mediawiki BIP47], Alice sends Bob her payment code once, in a ''notification transaction'', and both then derive the address of each payment from an ECDH between keys of their two payment codes.
Payment codes are meant to be published, so a CRQC that knows both codes derives every address of the relationship, whether or not the notification is protected.

HKE fits a new payment-code version in which Bob's payment code carries <code>ek</code> (about 1.3 KB instead of 80 bytes), the notification carries <code>c</code>, and each payment's secret is derived from <code>K</code> and a counter rather than from the payment codes.
One ciphertext per counterparty then protects every payment of the relationship.
Alice must store <code>K</code>, since she cannot recompute it from her seed, and an observer still sees that Alice notified Bob.

===Nostr===

In [https://github.com/nostr-protocol/nips/blob/master/44.md NIP-44], encrypted direct messages derive a conversation key from a static-static ECDH on <code>secp256k1</code> and publish the ciphertext to relays, which may keep it indefinitely.
The specification already records the gap: ''&quot;No post-quantum security: a powerful quantum computer would be able to decrypt the messages.&quot;''
Size is no obstacle, since relays typically accept events far larger than 1 KB, which makes this the least constrained application discussed here.
One adaptation is needed: NIP-44's conversation key is symmetric, satisfying <code>conv(a, B) == conv(b, A)</code>, and a KEM breaks that symmetry because only the recipient holds <code>msk</code>.
Either the sender retains <code>K</code>, or each party encapsulates to the other.

===Encryption to a known public key===

Wallet backups, PSETs and output descriptors can be encrypted to a public key the sender already holds.
With HKE, that key is the recipient's HKE public key <code>(B_bytes, ek)</code>, not a <code>secp256k1</code> key alone.
No protocol constrains the size or the frequency of such messages, so HKE applies directly, with <code>K</code> as the key of a symmetric cipher.

==Backwards Compatibility==

HKE is a new primitive and changes no existing protocol.

==Test Vectors and Reference Code==

A Python reference implementation of the Specification section is in [[elip-0222/hke.py|<code>elip-0222/hke.py</code>]], with one function per pseudocode definition.
It uses a vendored copy of the [https://github.com/secp256k1lab/secp256k1lab/ secp256k1lab] library at version 1.0.0 (commit [https://github.com/secp256k1lab/secp256k1lab/commit/44dc4bd893b8f03e621585e3bf255253e0e0fbfb 44dc4bd893b8f03e621585e3bf255253e0e0fbfb]), and [https://github.com/GiacomoPope/kyber-py <code>kyber-py</code>] 1.2.0 for ML-KEM-768, which passes NIST's ACVP test vectors for FIPS 203.
Test vectors are in [[elip-0222/vectors.json|<code>elip-0222/vectors.json</code>]] and are produced by [[elip-0222/gen_vectors.py|<code>elip-0222/gen_vectors.py</code>]] in the same directory.
Each vector lists its inputs under <code>given</code> and the values an implementation must compute under <code>expected</code>; failure vectors have no <code>expected</code>.
They cover:

* '''Valid:''' <code>msk</code>, <code>e</code> and <code>m</code> as inputs; <code>b</code>, <code>B</code>, <code>ek</code>, <code>dk</code>, <code>E_bytes</code>, <code>c</code>, <code>K_c</code>, <code>K_q</code> and <code>K</code> as outputs. They include points <code>B</code>, <code>E</code> and <code>e*B</code> whose x coordinate starts with a zero byte, which catch an x coordinate encoded in fewer than 32 bytes. They also include a <code>K_c</code> and a <code>K_q</code> that start with a zero byte, which catch an implementation that does not handle them as fixed 32-byte strings.
* '''Valid encapsulation:''' <code>B_bytes</code>, <code>ek</code>, <code>e</code> and <code>m</code> as inputs; <code>E_bytes</code>, <code>c</code>, <code>K_c</code>, <code>K_q</code> and <code>K</code> as outputs, for an <code>ek</code> that <code>KeyGen</code> does not produce but <code>KEM.CheckEK</code> accepts. In that <code>ek</code>:
** each 3-byte group packs <code>q - 1</code> next to <code>q - 2</code>, so that a decoder that reads a bit from the wrong place sees a coefficient <code>>= q</code>;
** the last 32 bytes are <code>0xff</code>.
* '''Implicit rejection:''' ciphertexts that decapsulate without error to a <code>K</code> different from the encapsulated one, with that <code>K</code> and its <code>K_c</code> and <code>K_q</code> given.
* '''Decapsulation failure:''' an invalid <code>E_bytes</code> or a wrong <code>|c|</code>. The invalid <code>E_bytes</code> include:
** the 65-byte uncompressed and hybrid encodings, which a general SEC1 parser such as <code>secp256k1_ec_pubkey_parse</code> accepts;
** the 32-byte x-only encoding of BIP340;
** 33 zero bytes, which BIP327 decodes as the point at infinity;
** <code>X >= p</code> where <code>X - p</code> is a valid x coordinate, which catches an implementation that reduces <code>X</code> modulo <code>p</code>.
* '''Encapsulation failure:''' one of the following inputs is invalid:
** <code>B_bytes</code>, with the same classes as <code>E_bytes</code>;
** <code>ek</code> with a wrong <code>|ek|</code>, including valid <code>ML-KEM-512</code> and <code>ML-KEM-1024</code> keys;
** <code>ek</code> with a coefficient <code>>= q</code> at the first and last position of each of the three polynomials, which covers both 12-bit halves of a 3-byte group;
** <code>e</code> outside <code>[1, n-1]</code>, including <code>n + 1</code>, which catches an implementation that reduces <code>e</code> modulo <code>n</code>;
** <code>m</code> with a wrong <code>|m|</code>.

No vector reaches the reduction modulo <code>n</code> or the <code>b = 0</code> failure in <code>expandDecapsulationKey</code>: each happens with probability at most about <code>2^-128</code>.

